<h1>Audio mixing</h1>
<p>Sound in DemiEngine runs through a mixer with named <strong>buses</strong>, so music, effects, voice, and UI can be balanced separately. Scripts play one-shots and loops through <code>demi.audio</code>, or attach playback to an entity with <code>demi.audio.source</code>.</p>

<h2>What a bus is</h2>
<p>A bus is a volume lane. Every sound you play is routed into one, and the lanes sum upward. The default tree is <code>master</code> at the top with <code>music</code>, <code>sfx</code>, <code>voice</code>, and <code>ui</code> underneath. Turning down <code>music</code> leaves footsteps and dialogue alone; turning down <code>master</code> quiets everything.</p>
<pre><code>local Audio = require("demi.audio")

Audio.play("asset://audio/skyforge_rune_collect", {
    bus = "sfx",
    volume = 0.8,
})</code></pre>
<p><code>Audio.play(uri, options)</code> starts a sound from an audio asset URI. The options table says how it plays (fields below). It returns an integer <strong>handle</strong> for the playing voice, or <code>0</code> when nothing could be started; pass a nonzero handle to <code>Audio.stop</code> when you need to end it early.</p>

<h2>Play options</h2>
<table>
  <thead><tr><th>Option</th><th>Type</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td><code>bus</code></td><td>string</td><td>Mixer lane: <code>music</code>, <code>sfx</code>, <code>voice</code>, <code>ui</code> (under <code>master</code>)</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>loop</code></td><td>boolean</td><td>Repeat until stopped</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>streaming</code></td><td>boolean</td><td>Stream from disk instead of keeping the whole clip resident (music and ambience)</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>volume</code></td><td>number</td><td>Per-sound loudness multiplier</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>pitch</code></td><td>number</td><td>Playback rate multiplier (1 = normal)</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>pan</code></td><td>number</td><td>Stereo placement for non-spatial sounds</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>spatial</code></td><td>string</td><td><code>none</code>, <code>2d</code>, or <code>3d</code>. Only <code>3d</code> enables backend spatialization; use <code>pan</code> for non-spatial stereo placement.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>attenuation</code></td><td>string</td><td>Distance falloff: <code>none</code>, <code>inverse</code>, <code>linear</code>, or <code>exponential</code></td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>x</code> / <code>y</code> / <code>z</code></td><td>numbers</td><td>World position for spatial sounds (scalars, like transforms)</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>min_distance</code> / <code>max_distance</code></td><td>numbers</td><td>Near and far distance bounds for spatial attenuation; the sound is not necessarily silent at <code>max_distance</code></td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>rolloff</code></td><td>number</td><td>Nonnegative distance-falloff factor</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>doppler</code></td><td>boolean</td><td>Enable the backend Doppler factor; current Lua and entity-source requests submit zero source/listener velocity, so transform motion alone does not produce a shift</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>delay</code></td><td>number</td><td>Seconds to wait before the sound starts</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>fade_in</code></td><td>number</td><td>Seconds to ramp volume up at the start</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>concurrency_group</code></td><td>string</td><td>Pool name limiting how many similar sounds play at once</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>max_voices</code></td><td>number</td><td>Cap for that concurrency group</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>voice_stealing</code></td><td>string</td><td>What to drop when the cap is hit: <code>reject</code> (refuse the new sound), <code>oldest</code>, or <code>quietest</code></td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>pause_with_game</code></td><td>boolean</td><td>Whether <code>Time.set_paused</code> also pauses this sound</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>
<p>Distances use world units and times use seconds. Omitted play options use runtime defaults (for example <code>sfx</code>, no loop, <code>spatial = "none"</code>, inverse attenuation, and game-pause enabled); an asset's <code>settings.streaming</code> can additionally force streaming.</p>
<pre><code>local Audio = require("demi.audio")

Audio.play("asset://audio/skyforge_wind", {
    bus = "music",
    loop = true,
    streaming = true,
    spatial = "3d",
    x = 0, y = 0, z = -12,
    min_distance = 4,
    max_distance = 40,
    fade_in = 2,
    pause_with_game = true,
})

Audio.play("asset://audio/skyforge_step", {
    bus = "sfx",
    spatial = "2d",
    concurrency_group = "steps",
    max_voices = 3,
    voice_stealing = "oldest",
})</code></pre>
<p>The first call is looping streamed wind in 3D at a world position, fading in over two seconds and pausing with the game clock. The second is a non-spatial footstep limited to three simultaneous voices: a fourth step steals the oldest one. Both <code>spatial = "none"</code> and <code>spatial = "2d"</code> leave backend 3D positioning disabled.</p>

<h2>Snapshots and transitions</h2>
<p>A snapshot is a saved mix — bus volumes for a moment like “underwater” or “low health”. Switching between snapshots can be instant or gradual.</p>
<pre><code>local Audio = require("demi.audio")

Audio.define_snapshot("skyforge_low_health", {
    music = 0.4,
    sfx = 0.9,
    voice = 1.0,
    ui = 1.0,
})

Audio.transition_snapshot("skyforge_low_health", 0.5)  -- blend over 0.5 s

-- swap the music track under the player with a crossfade:
local music = Audio.play("asset://audio/skyforge_normal", { bus = "music" })
local next = Audio.crossfade(music, "asset://audio/skyforge_low_health", 1.2)
-- crossfade already fades out music; stop(next) later if needed</code></pre>
<ul>
  <li><code>Audio.define_snapshot(name, buses)</code> — saves a bus-volume table under <code>name</code>. Returns nothing.</li>
  <li><code>Audio.transition_snapshot(name, seconds)</code> — blends the live mix toward that snapshot. Returns <code>true</code> when the transition started.</li>
  <li><code>Audio.crossfade(from_handle, asset_id, duration, bus?, loop?, streaming?)</code> — starts <code>asset_id</code> and fades it in while fading the voice <code>from_handle</code> out. The optional arguments default to <code>music</code>, <code>true</code>, and <code>true</code>. Returns the new voice handle, or <code>0</code> on failure.</li>
  <li><code>Audio.stop(handle)</code> — stops one playing voice. Returns <code>true</code> when stopped.</li>
</ul>
<p>Snapshot tables map bus names to volume numbers. Define them once at startup (in <code>on_start</code> or <code>on_create</code> of an audio director script) and switch during play. The crossfade starts a new asset; its second argument is an <code>asset://</code> ID, not a voice handle.</p>

<h2>Entity-attached playback</h2>
<p>An <code>AudioSource</code> component holds the clip and playback settings on the entity itself. Starting it makes the sound follow the entity and obey its position, which is what 3D world sounds need.</p>
<pre><code>local AudioSource = require("demi.audio.source")

local handle = AudioSource.play("skyforge_rune")   -- play the entity's attached source
AudioSource.stop("skyforge_rune")                  -- stop whatever this entity is playing</code></pre>
<ul>
  <li><code>AudioSource.play(entity_id)</code> — starts the entity's <code>AudioSource</code> clip at the entity's position, using the component's bus/volume/spatial settings. Returns an integer handle (or <code>0</code> when the entity has no source to play).</li>
  <li><code>AudioSource.stop(entity_id)</code> — stops that entity's playback. Returns <code>true</code> when stopped.</li>
</ul>
<p>For the listening side of 3D audio, an <code>AudioListener</code> component marks the entity the mixer hears from — usually the player or the camera. Only one listener matters at a time.</p>
<pre><code>local Entity = require("demi.entity")

Entity.add_component(Entity.find("skyforge_player"), "AudioListener", {})
-- from then on, 3D sounds are mixed relative to the player's position</code></pre>
<p><code>Entity.add_component(id, "AudioListener", fields)</code> attaches the listener; the fields table can be empty. Spatial distance attenuation uses the listener's position and the sound's <code>min_distance</code>, <code>max_distance</code>, <code>attenuation</code>, and <code>rolloff</code> settings. Doppler would require relative velocity, which these Lua and entity-source paths currently leave at zero.</p>

